SWARM

File: openOODA/SWARM.oot
How to use helper agents. Goal: get the work done without wasting tokens.

1. Right-size the model
  Light work (hygiene, line lock, grammar, one command): use a fast model.
  Heavy work (compiler logic, multi-file bugs, architecture): use a strong model.

2. Small ships
  Give each agent one tight job, usually one file.
  Stop at the goal or the first hard fail.
  Do not hand one agent a whole backend.

3. If stuck
  Probe with grep, tail, and a short command.
  Kill a rogue process.
  More agents on one stuck bug wastes tokens.

4. Isolation
  Concurrent writers must own different files.
  If two jobs touch the same file, use separate worktrees or do them in order.

5. Hostile check
  A QA helper hunts edge cases that would falsify the happy path.
  Script smokes live in ../ooda/scripts/ as .oo (not .oos). Read SCRIPTING.oot first.
  If a hole lets bad work pass: close it, stop, report.
  QA names the hole. A builder owns the rewrite.

6. The 12-agent phalanx
  Use this shape for a large change.

  Engineering
    DE1 DE2 — build the product change
    RT — C runtime and the emit boundary

  Adversarial
    PV1 PV2 — red-team the builders
    SEC — path, shell, and cap leaks
    MEM — copies, leaks, unbounded growth

  Integration
    INT — build and trace breaks
    HARN — tests for the new logic
    HYG — line lock and leftover files

  Command
    DOCS — boards match the code
    COORD — handoffs, sprint, rollup

  COORD names who owns which file before anyone writes.
